        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "071541e3-e659-523c-b1f4-eade008267f7",
        ":question/problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{2}{9}}=1?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
